WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.038
Be exact about FIPS.

2
00:00:02.038 --> 00:00:07.500
What CyberArk documents: the Trust Protection
Foundation supports Windows servers

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.270
with FIPS mode enabled,
through the Windows

4
00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:13.501
policy that says to use
FIPS-compliant algorithms.

5
00:00:13.501 --> 00:00:18.511
The installation guide also says to enable
it only if your industry requires it,

6
00:00:18.511 --> 00:00:23.825
and links Microsoft's post explaining why
Microsoft no longer recommends FIPS mode.

7
00:00:23.825 --> 00:00:28.538
For a federal deployment, decide
FIPS mode per server, deliberately.

8
00:00:28.538 --> 00:00:33.698
The database's secrets are encrypted
with an AES-256 software key kept

9
00:00:33.698 --> 00:00:38.325
in the registry, a key held in a
hardware security module, or both.

10
00:00:38.325 --> 00:00:39.412
What not to claim.

11
00:00:39.412 --> 00:00:43.501
I searched NIST's validated-modules
list on October twenty-second.

12
00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:45.862
Under the vendor name
Venafi there's nothing.

13
00:00:45.862 --> 00:00:52.178
Under CyberArk there are two FIPS 140-3
Level 1 certificates, number 5205,

14
00:00:52.178 --> 00:00:58.600
the CyberArk Cryptographic Module,
and number 5122, its Java counterpart.

15
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.709
Neither names the Trust Protection
Foundation or Certificate Manager,

16
00:01:02.709 --> 00:01:06.750
and I found no CyberArk statement
saying which module the product uses.

17
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.775
So don't cite either certificate for
this product without a vendor letter.

18
00:01:10.775 --> 00:01:15.776
There's also no current Common
Criteria certificate; the last one,

19
00:01:15.776 --> 00:01:21.425
for Trust Protection Platform 23.1, was
archived on July twenty-ninth, 2026.

20
00:01:21.425 --> 00:01:26.602
The defensible evidence path, and this
is my inference, is FIPS-mode Windows,

21
00:01:26.602 --> 00:01:29.525
which uses Microsoft's validated modules,

22
00:01:29.525 --> 00:01:33.700
plus the HSM's own CMVP certificate
for the database secrets.

23
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.038
One catch, which Part 3 covers:

24
00:01:36.038 --> 00:01:40.812
servers running the ACME service
can't be in FIPS mode at all.

25
00:01:40.812 --> 00:01:45.897
Sources: CyberArk 26.1
Installation and Upgrade Guide pp.

26
00:01:45.897 --> 00:01:50.658
49–55 (FIPS, encryption keys);
System Requirements (HSMs);

27
00:01:50.658 --> 00:01:58.232
System encryption keys overview; NIST CMVP
validated-modules search (Vendor Venafi;

28
00:01:58.232 --> 00:02:02.561
Vendor CyberArk) and
certificates #5205 and #5122;

29
00:02:02.561 --> 00:02:08.260
Common Criteria Portal certified
and archived product lists.
